{"subjectId":"c","id":"stack-queue","title":"Stack & Queue","group":"Data Structures & Algorithms in C","order":43,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <strong>stack</strong> is last-in-first-out (push/pop at one end); a <strong>queue</strong> is first-in-first-out (enqueue at the back, dequeue at the front). In C you build them on an array or a linked list.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack\"></i>Array-based stack</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\n#define CAP 64\n\ntypedef struct { char items[CAP]; int top; } Stack;   /* top = count */\n\nbool push(Stack *s, char c) { if (s-&gt;top == CAP) return false; s-&gt;items[s-&gt;top++] = c; return true; }\nbool pop(Stack *s, char *out) { if (s-&gt;top == 0) return false; *out = s-&gt;items[--s-&gt;top]; return true; }\n\nbool balanced(const char *expr) {\n    Stack s = {.top = 0};\n    for (; *expr; expr++) {\n        char c = *expr, open;\n        if (strchr(\"([{\", c)) {\n            if (!push(&amp;s, c)) return false;\n        } else if (strchr(\")]}\", c)) {\n            if (!pop(&amp;s, &amp;open)) return false;\n            if ((c == ')' &amp;&amp; open != '(') || (c == ']' &amp;&amp; open != '[') || (c == '}' &amp;&amp; open != '{'))\n                return false;\n        }\n    }\n    return s.top == 0;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *tests[] = {\"{a[b(c)d]e}\", \"(]\", \"((())\", \"f(x[0])\"};\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) printf(\"%-12s %s\\n\", tests[i], balanced(tests[i]) ? \"balanced\" : \"NOT balanced\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{a[b(c)d]e}  balanced\n(]           NOT balanced\n((())        NOT balanced\nf(x[0])      balanced</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-refresh\"></i>Circular-buffer queue</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;\n\n#define QCAP 4\n\ntypedef struct { int buf[QCAP]; int head, count; } Queue;\n\nbool enqueue(Queue *q, int v) {\n    if (q-&gt;count == QCAP) return false;               /* full */\n    q-&gt;buf[(q-&gt;head + q-&gt;count) % QCAP] = v;          /* wrap around */\n    q-&gt;count++;\n    return true;\n}\n\nbool dequeue(Queue *q, int *out) {\n    if (q-&gt;count == 0) return false;                  /* empty */\n    *out = q-&gt;buf[q-&gt;head];\n    q-&gt;head = (q-&gt;head + 1) % QCAP;\n    q-&gt;count--;\n    return true;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    Queue q = {.head = 0, .count = 0};\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++)\n        if (!enqueue(&amp;q, i * 10)) printf(\"queue full, dropped %d\\n\", i * 10);\n    int v;\n    dequeue(&amp;q, &amp;v); printf(\"got %d\\n\", v);\n    dequeue(&amp;q, &amp;v); printf(\"got %d\\n\", v);\n    enqueue(&amp;q, 60); enqueue(&amp;q, 70);                  /* reuses freed slots */\n    while (dequeue(&amp;q, &amp;v)) printf(\"%d \", v);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>queue full, dropped 50\ngot 10\ngot 20\n30 40 60 70</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Linked-list stack (unbounded)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\ntypedef struct SNode { int val; struct SNode *below; } SNode;\n\nvoid push(SNode **top, int v) {\n    SNode *n = malloc(sizeof *n);\n    if (!n) exit(1);\n    n-&gt;val = v; n-&gt;below = *top; *top = n;\n}\n\nint pop(SNode **top) {                     /* caller checks for empty */\n    SNode *n = *top;\n    int v = n-&gt;val;\n    *top = n-&gt;below;\n    free(n);\n    return v;\n}\n\n/* evaluate a postfix (RPN) expression of single digits */\nint eval_rpn(const char *s) {\n    SNode *st = NULL;\n    for (; *s; s++) {\n        if (*s &gt;= '0' &amp;&amp; *s &lt;= '9') { push(&amp;st, *s - '0'); continue; }\n        if (*s == ' ') continue;\n        int b = pop(&amp;st), a = pop(&amp;st);\n        push(&amp;st, *s == '+' ? a + b : *s == '-' ? a - b : *s == '*' ? a * b : a / b);\n    }\n    return pop(&amp;st);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", eval_rpn(\"3 4 + 2 *\"));      /* (3 + 4) * 2 */\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", eval_rpn(\"5 1 2 + 4 * + 3 -\"));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>14\n14</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Array</th><th>Linked list</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Push/pop, enqueue/dequeue</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td>Capacity</td><td>Fixed (or amortised O(1) with <code>realloc</code>)</td><td>Unbounded</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>Compact, cache-friendly</td><td>One <code>malloc</code> + pointer per element</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you implement a queue using two stacks?</summary><div><p>Push onto an <em>in</em> stack. To dequeue, if the <em>out</em> stack is empty, pop everything from <em>in</em> and push it onto <em>out</em> (reversing the order), then pop from <em>out</em>. Each element moves at most twice, so operations are amortised O(1).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you tell a full circular buffer from an empty one?</summary><div><p><code>head == tail</code> is ambiguous, so either keep a separate <code>count</code> (as above), or always leave one slot unused (<code>(tail + 1) % CAP == head</code> means full).</p></div></details></section>"}